From perseant@hhhh.org  Mon Dec 18 19:35:49 2000
From: Konrad Schroder <perseant@hhhh.org>
To: port-alpha@netbsd.org
Date: Mon, 18 Dec 2000 19:35:49 +0000
Subject: SFB Xserver

I've just committed patches to XalphaNetBSD to make it understand the sfb
(pmagb-b) framebuffer and lk[24]01 keyboard.  The acceleration enabled for
the sfb is equivalent to that for the tga (bitblt only) since its code is
pretty much a clone of the tga code.

In order to compile it, you will need rev 1.3 of
syssrc/sys/dev/tc/sfbreg.h, and to "cd /sys/dev/tc; make includes"
beforehand, otherwise it is a straightforward rebuild.

I've been running this on a DEC3000/400 with a TCWSCONS kernel, and
speedwise it feels slightly slow but is quite usable.  I'm not sure how
well (or even if) it works with a non-wscons kernel.

Let me know if I've inadvertently hosed something; otherwise, enjoy!

						Konrad Schroder
						perseant@hhhh.org

From root@ihack.net  Tue Dec 19 06:35:49 2000
From: Charles M. Hannum <root@ihack.net>
To: port-alpha@netbsd.org
Date: Tue, 19 Dec 2000 06:35:49 +0000
Subject: INITIALLY_LEVEL_TRIGGERED() lossage (DANGER WILL ROBINSON!)

So, anyone who was around when I tried to boot NetBSD on a PWS500
earlier this year would have recalled that this is the code that broke
it -- it caused the machine to panic at boot time.  Coupled with the
fact that resetting the state at shutdown time fitted the original
reason for the code, this is why I *explicitly removed it*.

If we're going to work around Multia `brain damage' (and the stray
interrupts are not really braindamage, except inasmuchas the kernel
shouldn't be bitching about them), could we *PLEASE* do it in a way
that's not going to screw other people over?

If someone would like to provide a *working* Alpha Multia, I could be
convinced to figure out the real origin of the stray interrupts and
fix it.

